Mr. Dursley almost ran out of the house. He brushed past a small man in a violet cloak who seemed to be lost, and didn’t give him a second thought as he hurried to his car. He drove to Grunnings, parked in his usual spot, and went into his office. As usual, he glanced at the morning mail and swore loudly. There, sitting in his chair and reading his reports, was the man in the violet cloak. Mr. Dursley decided the best thing to do was to pretend he hadn’t seen him.

“Morning,” he grunted, snatching up a report to hide behind. But the little man stepped around it and spoke in a high-pitched, excited voice. “Mr. Dursley? I’m here on official business. I’m from the Ministry of——”

Mr. Dursley made a choking noise and grabbed the man by his cloak, dragging him out of the office and slamming the door. He hissed, “What do you want? I’ve told you people——”

“Mr. Dursley,” said the little man seriously, “I have a message for you. You-Know-Who has fallen. It’s true. The Order of the Phoenix is passing the news. Everything’s in chaos——”

Mr. Dursley cut him off. “I don’t believe a word of it! I don’t know anything! I don’t want to hear it! Get out of my office, you freak!”

He strode back to his desk, only to find an owl sitting on his papers, pecking at a report. He nearly fainted. When he looked again, the office was empty, except for a small, grey, feathery thing on his desk—he was sure it was an owl feather—and an empty cup of tea that smelled strange.

Mr. Dursley was uneasy all morning. He kept glancing at his watch, wishing for lunchtime. He decided to go home for lunch to avoid any more strange people who might turn up. He drove home, carefully avoiding the street where he’d seen the cat the day before.

When he arrived, Petunia was standing at the door, looking pale. She rushed towards him as soon as she saw him.

“Vernon! Something’s happened! I’ve seen owls all morning, right over Privet Drive! And, and——” she lowered her voice, leaning in close, “the neighbours are saying they saw people in cloaks last night, in the middle of the night——”

Mr. Dursley grabbed her arm. “Stop! Petunia, stop!”

He pushed her inside and slammed the door, leaning against it, breathing heavily. He closed his eyes, trying to calm down. When he opened them, he saw a parcel on the mantelpiece in the corner of the living room. A large, brown-paper parcel. A note was attached to it, which read:

Mr. and Mrs. Dursley

(The Living Room)

Mr. Dursley stared at the parcel, then at Petunia. Petunia stared back. Neither of them moved. Finally, Mr. Dursley stepped forward, picked up the parcel, and turned it over. There was no stamp, no postmark, just the name. He tore off the wrapping paper. Inside was a thick, heavy, leather-bound book. On the cover, embossed in gold letters, were the words:

Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry

(A History)

Mr. Dursley felt dizzy. He opened the book. It was full of strange words he couldn’t understand, like “Transfiguration,” “Potions,” and “Defence Against the Dark Arts”... He snapped the book shut and threw it onto the sofa, as if it might burn him.

“Petunia,” he said, his voice hoarse, “we have to get away from here. We have to go far away. We can’t let Harry——”

He didn’t finish. At that moment, there was a loud, urgent knocking at the front door.